  "title": "How to Simplify Algebraic Expressions",
  "content": "<p><strong>Simplifying</strong> means collecting like terms and reducing an expression to its shortest form. Like terms have the same letter raised to the same power — for example \\(3x\\) and \\(5x\\) are like terms, but \\(3x\\) and \\(3x^2\\) are not.</p><p>When <strong>collecting like terms</strong>, group all the terms with the same variable and power together, then add or subtract the coefficients. Constants (plain numbers) are also like terms with each other.</p><p>When <strong>multiplying terms</strong>, multiply the coefficients and add the indices: \\(3x^2 \\times 4x^3 = 12x^5\\). When <strong>dividing</strong>, divide the coefficients and subtract the indices: \\(12x^5 \\div 4x^2 = 3x^3\\).</p><p>Remember the index laws: \\(x^a \\times x^b = x^{a+b}\\), \\(x^a \\div x^b = x^{a-b}\\), and \\((x^a)^b = x^{ab}\\). Any term raised to the power 0 equals 1.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Simplify</strong> \\(5x + 3y - 2x + 7y\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Like terms: \\(5x\\) and \\(-2x\\); \\(3y\\) and \\(7y\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Group: \\((5x - 2x) + (3y + 7y)\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Collect: \\(3x + 10y\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(3x + 10y\\)</p>"
 },
